**What each covers:**

**`freshwater-fish`** — River/lake fish (grass carp, pike, sport fish, sardines). Distinctly different Hg/As contamination pathway from marine fish: sediment-driven rather than oceanic bioaccumulation. Sources span Brazil (Tapajós), Canada (Mackenzie), Latvia, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Thailand, Tanzania. Sibling of `fish.md`.

**`products/seafood`** — Consumer-facing seafood product category. `ingredients/seafood.md` already exists as the raw commodity page; this would be the regulatory/advisory view: FDA/EPA fish consumption advisories, EU limits, MeHg species selection guidance.

**`cereals`** — Codex/EFSA/JECFA formal grain category (wheat, barley, rye, oats, maize, including rice) used in maximum-level setting. All 8 sources are regulatory: Codex CXS-193, EFSA cadmium/lead/nickel opinions, JECFA cadmium assessment. Would anchor the regulatory grain pages; existing `wheat.md`, `oat.md`, `non-rice-grains.md` link from it.

**`shellfish`** — Bivalves + crustaceans (broader than `bivalve-molluscs.md`). Crustacean hepatopancreas has a notably different Cd profile than bivalves. FDA/EPA fish consumption advice uses shellfish as a distinct MeHg advisory matrix. Would be parent to `bivalve-molluscs.md`.

**`breastmilk`** — Human breast milk as an infant exposure matrix. The key low-arsenic comparator in the formula/breastmilk arsenic literature. Thoerig 2025 is a 2025 systematic review specifically on toxic elements in human milk vs. formula. Carignan 2015/2016 are the canonical breastfeeding/formula arsenic cohort papers.

**`quinoa`** — Andean grain with distinct Cd pathway: high-altitude Peruvian soils are naturally elevated in Cd and quinoa concentrates it differently from other grains. All 6 sources specifically measure quinoa. Includes the Consumer Reports 2014 grain survey, which found quinoa had significantly lower iAs than rice.

